<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">David Hume</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1776">1776</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hummay">Hume</a> was a Scottish philosopher, historian and essayist, one of the leading figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a>. His Treatise of Human Nature and later Enquiries argued that knowledge rests on experience and habit, raising radical sceptical questions about causation and religion. His six-volume History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was a best-seller, and he was a close friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david-hume.jpg" alt="David Hume" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allan Ramsay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Hume_Ramsay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Scottish Enlightenment flourished after the union: <b>David Hume</b>, Adam Smith, whose Wealth of Nations appeared in 1776, and Adam Ferguson shaped modern philosophy, economics and social science, while Samuel Johnson’s Dictionary (1755) and the novels of Daniel Defoe, Samuel Richardson and Henry Fielding helped establish modern English…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Scotland <b>David Hume</b>, sceptic and historian of England, and Adam Smith, professor at Glasgow and author of The Theory of Moral Sentiments and The Wealth of Nations, turned moral philosophy towards the study of society.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In the mid-18th century <b>David Hume</b> and Adam Smith challenged the mercantilist belief that the world’s wealth was fixed and one state could grow rich only at another’s expense.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Adam Smith, <b>David Hume</b>, Edward Gibbon, Voltaire and Jean-Jacques Rousseau are counted the founding fathers of anti-mercantilist thought.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1753" aria-label="Edinburgh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4481 258 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of David Hume"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1753">What was here in 1753? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Does it contain any abstract reasoning concerning quantity or number? No. Does it contain any experimental reasoning concerning matter of fact and existence? No. Commit it then to the flames: for it can contain nothing but sophistry and illusion.&#8221;</p><footer>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, section 12 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Custom, then, is the great guide of human life. It is that principle alone which renders our experience useful to us, and makes us expect, for the future, a similar train of events with those which have appeared in the past.&#8221;</p><footer>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, section 5 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions, and can never pretend to any other office than to serve and obey them.&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise of Human Nature, book 2, part 3, section 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4705/pg4705-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A wise man, therefore, proportions his belief to the evidence.&#8221;</p><footer>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, section 10 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Be a philosopher; but, amidst all your philosophy, be still a man.&#8221;</p><footer>An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding, section 1 &#183; original &#183; the counsel he gives in the voice of Nature &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9662/pg9662-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is not contrary to reason to prefer the destruction of the whole world to the scratching of my finger.&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise of Human Nature, book 2, part 3, section 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4705/pg4705-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
